19	 *     per invocation, so tests can assert on exact commands issued (e.g.
20	 *     the body of a posted comment), not just the script's own stdout.
21	 *
22	 * A rule may also carry `attempt: N`, meaning it applies only the Nth time a call
23	 * matching THAT rule is made -- not the Nth `gh` invocation overall. Counting per
24	 * rule rather than globally is what keeps such a test from breaking when unrelated
25	 * `gh` traffic appears before it. That is what makes a retry testable: attempt 1
26	 * returns the rate limit and the next returns the answer. The count comes from
27	 * FAKE_GH_LOG, so a test using `attempt` must set it -- each invocation is its own
28	 * process and the log is the only state they share.
